Here's the final program:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata;

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions(JsonSerializerDefaults.Web)
{
    TypeInfoResolver = new DefaultJsonTypeInfoResolver()
};

// 1) Strongly-typed: GetTypeInfo<T>() → JsonTypeInfo<T>
JsonTypeInfo<Person> personInfo = options.GetTypeInfo<Person>();
string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new Person("Alice", 30), personInfo);

// 2) Probe without throwing: TryGetTypeInfo(Type, out JsonTypeInfo?)
if (options.TryGetTypeInfo(typeof(Person), out JsonTypeInfo? info)) { /* use it */ }

// With an empty resolver → returns false, no exception
var empty = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    TypeInfoResolver = JsonTypeInfoResolver.Combine()
};
empty.TryGetTypeInfo(typeof(Person), out _); // false

record Person(string Name, int Age);
```

**Key APIs:**

| Goal | Method |
|------|--------|
| Get `JsonTypeInfo<T>` directly | `options.GetTypeInfo<T>()` |
| Check without throwing | `options.TryGetTypeInfo(typeof(T), out var info)` |

`GetTypeInfo<T>()` throws if no resolver can provide metadata; `TryGetTypeInfo` returns `false` instead — perfect for branching logic.
